<section xml:id="ws-mod2-video-outlines">
  <title>Module 2: Video Lecture Outlines</title>
  <subtitle>Instructor Reference — Two Lightboard Videos</subtitle>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      These outlines are for the instructor's use in preparing the
      lightboard recordings. Each video runs approximately 8&#x2013;9
      minutes. Students are directed to watch the videos before completing
      the companion reading and worked examples.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================
       VIDEO 1
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-vid1">
    <title>Video 1: Absolute Value and the Triangle Inequality (~8 min)</title>
    <p><em>Source: Zorn §1.7</em></p>
    <p><em>Core message: Absolute value measures distance. The Triangle
    Inequality is the one rule governing how distances add. You will use
    both every time you write an analysis proof.</em></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-vid1-outline">
    <title>Outline</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Opening (1 min)</alert>
      Write on board: <m>|x - a|</m>. Ask students: what does this
      measure? Answer: the distance between <m>x</m> and <m>a</m> on the
      number line. Emphasize that this is the <em>only</em> way to
      measure distance in <m>\R</m>, so every convergence definition
      will pass through it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Key properties (2 min)</alert>
      State and briefly illustrate (no formal proof) the properties
      from Theorem 1.23:
    </p>
    <p>
      (a) <m>-|x| \leq x \leq |x|</m> (x is always sandwiched between
      its absolute value and its negative)
    </p>
    <p>
      (b) <m>|xy| = |x||y|</m> (absolute value distributes over
      multiplication)
    </p>
    <p>
      (c) <m>|x| \lt k \iff -k \lt x \lt k</m> (the conversion rule:
      an absolute value inequality is an interval). Write a concrete
      example: <m>|x - 3| \lt 2</m> means <m>1 \lt x \lt 5</m>,
      i.e., <m>x</m> is within 2 units of 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>The Triangle Inequality (3 min)</alert>
      State: <m>|x + y| \leq |x| + |y|</m> for all <m>x, y \in \R</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Demonstrate with a picture: draw a number line, mark <m>0</m>,
      <m>x</m>, and <m>x + y</m>. The total displacement <m>|x + y|</m>
      is at most the sum of the individual displacements <m>|x|</m> and
      <m>|y|</m> (equality holds when <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> have the
      same sign; the inequality is strict when they partially cancel).
    </p>
    <p>
      Show three applications on the board:
    </p>
    <p>
      (1) <m>|a - b| \leq |a - c| + |c - b|</m> (write
      <m>a - b = (a-c) + (c-b)</m> and apply TI directly).
    </p>
    <p>
      (2) Bounding a sum: if <m>|x - 3| \lt 1</m> and
      <m>|y - 3| \lt 1</m>, how big can <m>|x + y - 6|</m> be?
      Answer: <m>|x + y - 6| = |(x-3) + (y-3)| \leq |x-3| + |y-3| \lt 2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      (3) Preview the <m>\varepsilon</m>-<m>N</m> context: to show
      <m>|a_n - L| \lt \varepsilon</m>, we will routinely write
      <m>a_n - L = (a_n - M) + (M - L)</m> and bound each piece
      separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>The reverse triangle inequality (1 min)</alert>
      State without proof: <m>\bigl||x| - |y|\bigr| \leq |x - y|</m>.
      Give intuition: the difference in distances is at most the distance
      between the points. This comes up when bounding expressions from
      below. The full proof is in the Worked Examples.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Closing (1 min)</alert>
      Reiterate: the single most important skill from this video is
      recognizing when and how to split an expression and apply the
      Triangle Inequality. Students will practice this in the worked
      examples and practice set.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

  <!-- ============================================================
       VIDEO 2
  ============================================================ -->

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-vid2">
    <title>Video 2: Bounds, Suprema, and the Completeness Axiom (~9 min)</title>
    <p><em>Source: Zorn §1.8&#x2013;1.9</em></p>
    <p><em>Core message: The real number system is complete: every nonempty
    set bounded above has a least upper bound in <m>\R</m>. The rationals
    lack this property, and that gap is why we need <m>\R</m>.</em></p>
  </paragraphs>

  <paragraphs xml:id="par-mod2-vid2-outline">
    <title>Outline</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Opening: the gap in <m>\Q</m> (2 min)</alert>
      Write on board: <m>T = \{x \in \Q : x^2 \lt 2\}</m>. Ask:
      does this set have a least upper bound in <m>\Q</m>? Walk
      through the reasoning: <m>T</m> is nonempty and bounded above
      in <m>\Q</m>, but every candidate rational upper bound either
      has its square strictly greater than 2 (and we can squeeze
      in a smaller one) or equals <m>\sqrt{2}</m>, which is not in
      <m>\Q</m>. So <m>T</m> has no supremum in <m>\Q</m>: there is
      a genuine <em>gap</em> at <m>\sqrt{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Punchline: we build <m>\R</m> precisely to fill all such gaps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Upper bounds and the supremum (2 min)</alert>
      Define:
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>U</m> is an <em>upper bound</em> of <m>S</m> if
      <m>x \leq U</m> for all <m>x \in S</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>s = \sup S</m> if (i) <m>s</m> is an upper bound, and
      (ii) <m>s \leq U</m> for every upper bound <m>U</m>
      (i.e., <m>s</m> is the <em>smallest</em> upper bound).
    </p>
    <p>
      Quick example on the board:
      <m>S = \{1 - 1/n : n \in \N\} = \{0, 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, \ldots\}</m>.
      The values are increasing toward 1 but never reach it. So
      <m>1</m> is an upper bound; is it the least? Yes, because for
      any <m>t \lt 1</m>, we can find an element of <m>S</m> larger
      than <m>t</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>The <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization (2 min)</alert>
      Restate the supremum condition (ii) in a more useful form:
      <m>s = \sup S</m> if and only if <m>s</m> is an upper bound
      and for every <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m> there exists
      <m>x \in S</m> with <m>s - \varepsilon \lt x</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Illustrate with <m>S = \{1 - 1/n\}</m>: given <m>\varepsilon \gt 0</m>,
      we need <m>n</m> large enough so that <m>1 - 1/n \gt 1 - \varepsilon</m>,
      i.e., <m>1/n \lt \varepsilon</m>. The Archimedean Property
      guarantees such <m>n</m> exists. This is exactly the template
      for the worked example.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>The Completeness Axiom (2 min)</alert>
      State the axiom: every nonempty subset of <m>\R</m> that is
      bounded above has a supremum in <m>\R</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Point out that this is an <em>axiom</em>, not a theorem&#x2014;we
      accept it as a defining property of <m>\R</m>, not something we
      prove from more basic facts. It is the axiom that distinguishes
      <m>\R</m> from <m>\Q</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Apply it to the gap example: the Completeness Axiom guarantees
      that <m>\sup T = \sqrt{2}</m> exists in <m>\R</m>, even though
      it does not exist in <m>\Q</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Consequences (1 min)</alert>
      Briefly name two corollaries students will see in the Bridge
      Reading (Bauldry): the Archimedean Property (for every
      <m>x \in \R</m> there exists <m>n \in \N</m> with <m>n \gt x</m>)
      and the density of <m>\Q</m> in <m>\R</m> (between any two reals
      there is a rational). Both follow from completeness. Both
      will be used throughout MAT 5610.
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Closing (30 sec)</alert>
      Completeness is a structural fact about <m>\R</m>, and the
      <m>\varepsilon</m>-characterization is the tool for applying it
      in proofs. The worked example shows the full proof template.
    </p>
  </paragraphs>

</section>
